      "body": "### Environment\n\nNode version: v22.19.0\nnpm version: 11.17.0\nLocal ESLint version: v10.8.0\nGlobal ESLint version: N/A\nOperating System: macOS Darwin 25.5.0\n\n### What parser are you using?\n\nDefault (Espree)\n\n### What did you do?\n\n**Configuration**\n\n```js\nexport default [\n  {\n    rules: {\n      \"no-unused-labels\": \"error\"\n    }\n  }\n];\n```\n\n**Code**\n\n```js\nfunction foo() {}\nfoo()\nLABEL: [1, 2, 3].forEach(x => x)\n```\n\nRun ESLint with `--fix` flag or apply the autofix from the editor.\n\n### What did you expect to happen?\n\nNo autofix should be applied, because removing the label would create an ASI hazard \u2014 the two statements would be merged into one by the JS engine.\n\n### What actually happened?\n\nThe autofix removes `LABEL: ` and produces:\n\n```js\nfunction foo() {}\nfoo()\n[1, 2, 3].forEach(x => x)\n```\n\nThis is parsed as a **single statement**:\n\n```js\nfoo()[1, 2, 3].forEach(x => x)\n```\n\nwhich is semantically completely different from the original code.\n\nThe same issue occurs when the label body starts with `(`, `/`, `+`, `-`, or a template literal (`` ` ``).\n\n### Link to Minimal Reproducible Example\n\nhttps://eslint.org/play/#eyJ0ZXh0IjoiLyogZXNsaW50IG5vLXVudXNlZC1sYWJlbHM6IFwiZXJyb3JcIiAqL1xuZnVuY3Rpb24gZm9vKCkge31cbmZvbygpXG5MQUJFTDogWzEsIDIsIDNdLmZvckVhY2goeCA9PiB4KSIsIm9wdGlvbnMiOnsicnVsZXMiOnsibm8tdW51c2VkLWxhYmVscyI6WyJlcnJvciJdfSwibGFuZ3VhZ2VPcHRpb25zIjp7InBhcnNlck9wdGlvbnMiOnsiZWNtYUZlYXR1cmVzIjp7fX19fX0=\n\n### Participation\n\n- [x] I am willing to submit a pull request for this issue.\n\n### AI acknowledgment\n\n- [x] I did not use AI to generate this issue report.\n- [ ] (If the above is not checked) I have reviewed the AI-generated content before submitting.\n\n### Additional comments\n\nIssue #16988 / PR #17314 previously added a guard to prevent autofix when removing the label would turn the body into a directive (string literal). However, isFixable() still does not check for ASI hazards caused by tokens like [, (, `, /, +, -.\n\nThe no-console rule already handles this pattern correctly via its maybeAsiHazard() function (see lib/rules/no-console.js).",
