      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "### ESLint version\n\nv10.8.0\n\n### What problem do you want to solve?\n\nIn multithread mode (--concurrency), files are distributed to workers in glob traversal order via a shared atomic counter (Atomics.add).\n\nWhen projects contain files with significantly different lint times\u2014such as AI-generated, ORM, or auto-generated code mixed with normal source files\u2014workers that finish small files early sit idle while a single worker processes a large file alone (\"straggler\" / Long-Tail problem).\n\nWhile ESLintPoorConcurrencyWarning already detects this symptom, it only fires after the run with no corrective action.\n\nBenchmark Setup (200 small files + 1 large AI-generated file with 11,662 lines, 5 workers):\nCurrent (Glob Order): 844ms\n\n### What do you think is the correct solution?\n\nImplement Longest Processing Time First (LPT) sorting before distributing tasks to workers:\n\n1. Store per-file execution time in the existing lint result cache (meta.lintTime).\n2. Sort files by cached lintTime in descending order prior to worker dispatch.\n3. Fall back to stat.size on the first run (since fsp.stat is already called during file discovery with a ~0.7ms overhead) or when --cache is disabled.\n\nBenchmark Results:\n- Cache LPT (lintTime-based): 621ms (26.5% faster)\n- stat.size LPT (fallback): 613ms (27.4% faster)\n\n### Participation\n\n- [x] I am willing to submit a pull request for this change.\n\n### AI acknowledgment\n\n- [x] I did not use AI to generate this issue report.\n- [ ] (If the above is not checked) I have reviewed the AI-generated content before submitting.\n\n### Additional comments\n\n_No response_",
